3. Responsibilities
Vehicle hierarchy: holds the registration number and knows its own hourly rate and fee calculation. It does not know about slots or tickets.
ParkingSlot: knows its position, kind (regular or charging) and occupant, and refuses a second vehicle.
ParkingTicket: holds a unique ticket number, vehicle, slot, entry time and exit time. It is immutable once issued, except for the exit time.
ParkingFacility: finds a suitable free slot, issues tickets, releases slots and reports occupancy. It delegates fee logic to the vehicle.
MonthlyPass / UserCategory: decide whether the fee is waived.

This follows the single-responsibility principle. If the fee rules change, only the vehicle classes change, and if the slot layout changes, only the facility does.

  
4. Business rules
A slot can hold at most one vehicle at a time.
A vehicle cannot be parked twice at once, so the registration number must be unique among active tickets.
Ticket numbers are unique and never reused, even after the ticket is closed.
A ticket is issued only if a suitable slot was allocated.
Only an occupied slot can be released, and slot positions must be valid (floor and slot within bounds).
Exit time must not be before entry time.
Every started hour is charged in full, so 61 minutes is billed as 2 hours.
Staff and students with a valid monthly pass pay no parking fee. An expired pass or a visitor pays the full fee.
Only EVs can use charging slots, and charging is billed separately by kWh.
A non-EV may not take a charging slot.

  
5. Assumptions
Vehicle types are two-wheeler, car and EV, and slots are numbered from 0 within each floor.
The entry time is taken from the system clock or supplied as input, and times fall within one day for the basic version.
Allocation is first-free (lowest floor, then lowest slot), and a vehicle that can’t be placed is turned away.
A ticket is not reissued after exit, and a lost ticket is looked up by registration number.
Pass validity is checked against the entry date.
The facility size is fixed at construction.

  
6. Alternative concept: parallel arrays of Strings
java
// Parallel-array design
String[] regNo     = new String[MAX];
String[] type      = new String[MAX];
String[] entryTime = new String[MAX];
int count = 0;

// Add
regNo[count] = "TN01AB1111"; type[count] = "car"; entryTime[count] = "09:30"; count++;

// Find by registration number
int find(String r) {
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        if (regNo[i].equals(r)) return i;
    return -1;
}
java
// Object design
Vehicle[] vehicles = new Vehicle[MAX];
Vehicle find(String r) {
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        if (vehicles[i].getRegNo().equals(r)) return vehicles[i];
    return null;
}
Aspect	Parallel arrays	Vehicle objects
Search by reg no	Linear scan, O(n) comparisons	Linear scan, O(n) comparisons (same, with an extra getRegNo() call)
Add an attribute (e.g. owner)	Add a new array and update every insert, delete and shift so indices stay aligned. Miss one and the data silently corrupts.	Add one field and a getter to the class. Other code is untouched.
Type safety	type[] is a free String (“car” vs “Car” vs “cr”), and entry time as a String can’t be computed on directly	Types are checked by the compiler, and behaviour (calculateFee) can live with the data
Per-vehicle space	3 references, about 12 bytes with compressed pointers, plus the Strings themselves	1 reference plus an object header and 3 fields, roughly 12 to 16 bytes more per vehicle

Complexity:

Searching is O(n) in both designs, or O(n·L) if you count string comparison cost for registration numbers of length L. The design alone doesn’t change this. Fast lookup needs a different structure, such as a HashMap, covered in Task 11.
Space per vehicle is O(1) in both designs, and O(n) in total. Objects pay a small constant overhead for the object header, and in return you get safety, encapsulation and polymorphism. The byte figures are approximate for a 64-bit JVM with compressed pointers and depend on your JVM.
Adding an attribute costs O(1) design effort with objects, but with parallel arrays it touches every place that keeps the arrays in sync, so effort grows with code size and the risk of misalignment is high.

Conclusion to write: the objects cost a few extra bytes per vehicle but give the same search time, safer changes, and a natural home for behaviour such as fee calculation, which is why the object design is retained.

  
